          <tr><th>Confidence</th><td>High — customs/MIIT production and export data</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>


      <h2>China's 2024 shipments by segment</h2>
      <p>Reported by the China Lead-Acid Battery Industry Association, based on registered-enterprise statistics:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>2024 volume</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>E-bike batteries</td><td>~167.7 GWh</td><td>Largest single segment in the Chinese lead-acid market</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Automotive SLI batteries</td><td>~107.0 GWh</td><td>37 registered enterprises reported</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stationary / standby batteries</td><td>~9.1 GWh</td><td>11 stationary-battery manufacturers reported</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total shipments</strong></td><td><strong>413 GWh</strong></td><td>USD 37.7 bn turnover; +10.6% YoY</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Exports</h2>
      <p>
        Reported customs/industry data put China's lead-acid battery exports at roughly
        <strong>78.4 million units with an export value of about USD 4.12 billion (2025)</strong>. Export value is a
        small fraction of total turnover — most Chinese lead-acid output is consumed domestically, which is itself a
        signal of how much of the market is internal automotive and e-bike demand.
      </p>

      <h2>Global context</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Global lead-acid market, 2025</td><td>USD 50.9 bn</td><td>Fortune Business Insights</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Asia-Pacific share, 2025</td><td>39.6% (USD 20.16 bn)</td><td>Fortune Business Insights</td></tr>
          <tr><td>China market (2026 projected)</td><td>USD 11.9 bn</td><td>Fortune Business Insights</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>口径 (scope) — read before trusting any number</h2>
      <p>
        "Lead-acid production" is not one number. Sources differ because they measure different things — GWh
        (energy shipped) versus units versus value, automotive-only versus all applications, and new versus recycled.
        The 413 GWh figure above is an <em>energy-shipment</em> measure across all segments including e-bike; the
        export figure is a <em>unit-and-value</em> measure. Do not mix the two scopes. See the
        <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> for how this site flags those differences.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My position:</strong> The lead-acid story is a volume-and-scale story, not a technology story — and the
        e-bike share of China's shipments is the clearest evidence of it.</p>
        <p><strong>Why I think so:</strong> A mature, commodity market with an e-bike battery as its largest single segment
        tells you everything about lead-acid economics: thin margins, large volumes, and a domestic market that dwarfs
        its exports.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not investment or purchasing advice.</em></p>
